    description: "## Origin\n\nMaterialized 2026-06-04 from the 2026-06-03 fold-audit (`~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/notebookjournal_06022026_2100_fold_audit_passes_21-40.md`, Family A). Consolidates F40.A1 + F40.A2 previously folded onto INV-titid. Full evidence remains on the INV-titid discussion entry; this row provides discrete representation.\n\n## Symptom\n\nThe `compact` writer emits a behavior map whose top-level summary fields describe the SOURCE map rather than the projected view:\n\n**F40.A1 — metrics block echoes pre-projection totals.** On a compact substrate containing 100 nodes / 370 edges / 33 EPs (default `--max-symbols 100`), the metrics block reports `metrics.total_nodes=34253`, `metrics.total_edges=109915`, `metrics.total_files=915` — the SOURCE map's counts. `metrics.by_supply_chain_tier.first_party.nodes=32531 / edges=109892` similarly carries source-side numbers. A consumer reading compact.json's metrics block to understand \"what's in this file\" gets the source size, not the view size.\n\n**F40.A2 — analysis_incomplete=false despite 99.7% truncation.** Compact substrate with `nodes_summary.omitted.count=34153` (99.71% of source nodes dropped) and 71/104 EPs dropped reports `analysis_incomplete=false`. The flag tracks analyzer-side failures only, not view-side projection. A downstream consumer using `analysis_incomplete` to decide whether to trust `metrics` gets False on a dramatically incomplete view.\n\n## Invariant\n\nWhen `view==compact` (or any projection writer), top-level summary fields that describe extent or completeness must describe the projected output, not the source. Three concrete sub-invariants:\n\n1. `metrics.total_nodes == len(nodes)`, `metrics.total_edges == len(edges)`, `metrics.total_files == len(unique_paths)` in the projected output.\n2. `metrics.by_supply_chain_tier.<tier>.{nodes,edges}` reflect counts of the projected node/edge set, not the source.\n3. `analysis_incomplete=true` when ANY top-level array has been view-truncated, OR a peer `view_truncated` boolean exists and is set.\n\n## Fix sketch\n\n(a) Recompute the affected `metrics` fields from the post-projection arrays inside the compact writer, OR (b) flip `analysis_incomplete=true` whenever a projection writer truncates ANY top-level array, OR (c) leave analyzer-scope fields untouched and add a separate `view_truncation` peer block (cousin to WI-compact-truncation-marker — both fixes could share that block). The cleanest split is to keep `analysis_incomplete` as analyzer-scope, recompute `metrics.total_*` from the projected arrays, and let the new `view_truncation` block (per WI-compact-truncation-marker) carry the per-array projection signal.\n\n## Source provenance\n\n- **F40.A1 + F40.A2** — INV-titid discussion entry 2026-06-03T00:10:06 (pass-40 batch; see paragraphs beginning \"F40.A1 (metrics-echo)\" and \"F40.A2 (analysis_incomplete flag)\").\n\nLab notebook: pass 40 under `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/`.\n\n## Related rows\n\n- Parent: **INV-fanur** (META: Sketch budget enforcement layered without coordinator)\n- Sibling: **INV-pazur** (nodes_summary pre-shrink — closest existing self-description gap)\n- Sibling: **WI-compact-truncation-marker** (Family A, per-array projection markers)\n- Sibling: **WI-compact-selection-non-monotonic** (Family A)\n- Sibling: **WI-compact-induced-subgraph-incomplete** (Family A)"  # e84b
    fields: {}  # e84b
